JavaScript, JSX, and TSX list destructuring declarations as TypeScript does: the pattern prints as written after the keywords. In JavaScript that includes a `using` declaration, which the TypeScript grammars parse as an error (ADR-008).
== args
input/destructuring.js
input/destructuring.jsx
input/destructuring.tsx
== file input/destructuring.js
export const { a, b: renamed } = load()
let [x, , z] = point
using { handle } = open()
== file input/destructuring.jsx
const { title } = props
== file input/destructuring.tsx
const [state, setState] = useState(0)
== stdout
input/destructuring.js (3 lines)
[L1-L1] export const { a, b: renamed }
[L2-L2] let [x, , z]
[L3-L3] using { handle }

input/destructuring.jsx (1 line)
[L1-L1] const { title }

input/destructuring.tsx (1 line)
[L1-L1] const [state, setState]
== stderr
== exit 0
